>> Since when is bash *not* a posix shell? I use that *exact* syntax at
>> least weekly with bash 2.04. 

It's an optional (unnecessary and incompatible) bash feature, not a
feature of a POSIX shell. There are a couple shells that conform to
POSIX, i.e. shells that support all the features defined by the POSIX
standard. Individual shell's development is free to incorporate any
feature they like, but "Any Feature" won't run on other POSIX shells.
There are some extensions that are established and have been added to
serveral POSIX shells but are not POSIX themself. Other features are
unique. If you want to program portably the most rewarding approach
is to program POSIX conformant. If you need "more powerful" functions
then use the "common extensions" that are implemented in the prevalent
shells, bash and ksh (ksh88 to be more precise), on Linux and on the
commercial Unix systems. But never rely on shell specific extensions.

